{"purpose":"Define how a device learns who is reachable right now, so that coordination and emergency use cases do not begin with a message sent into a void.","content":"## Requirement\n\nA device must be able to see which other devices are online, each device's custom status message, and when a device that is offline was last seen.\n\n## Behaviour\n\n- **Online / offline** state per device.\n- **Custom status message**, set by the user, retained across reconnects.\n- **Last seen** timestamp for offline devices.\n\n## The design constraint that must not be relaxed\n\nPresence is **server-authoritative with a liveness TTL**. The coordinator derives online state from a heartbeat it observes, and transitions a device to offline when the heartbeat lapses.\n\nClient-announced presence is explicitly rejected: a device that crashes, loses power, or is killed cannot send a goodbye, and would therefore remain 'online' indefinitely. In an emergency-communication tool, a stale 'online' indicator is worse than no indicator at all, because it invites someone to rely on a device that is not there.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n1. A device that connects appears online to every other connected device.\n2. A device that disconnects cleanly appears offline, with a last-seen timestamp.\n3. A device whose process is killed without warning appears offline within the liveness TTL, without having announced anything.\n4. A custom status set by a user survives that user's reconnect.\n5. Presence works on a build compiled without the audio build tag."}